{"id":"I1","title":"Exact score attribution and controlled ablation","summary":"Audit explicit scoring computation: reconstruct contributions at frozen transforms, then ablate terms to test rank sensitivity; MEGADOCK exposes scoring variants and weights (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/akiyamalab/MEGADOCK/master/doc/README.md). Diagnostic proposal written to interp.md.","root_link":"Addresses dense-search best50 gains versus rank1 losses, without changing frozen replication arms: file:///home/hal/dev/interp/metis/experiments/blind-02/20261103-0124-tree/out/work/seed-20261103/R1/framer.md.","compute_open":"none","sequential":"Proposed audit needs baseline plus three ablations per case/arm across four cases and three arms; frozen-pose calculations split independently and CPU analysis uses zero GPU capacity. Full-search variants retain roughly five dependent stages per rotation across 3,600 or 54,000 rotations, but rotations already support concurrent OpenMP execution; instrumented GPU busy fraction remains unknown: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/akiyamalab/MEGADOCK/master/doc/README.md."}
{"id":"I2","title":"Differential tracing of CPU/GPU numerical disagreement","summary":"Compare intermediate arrays to locate first divergence, then test independent numerical fixtures; reassociation and fused multiply-add can change compliant floating-point results (https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/floating-point/index.html). Tracing supports diagnosis, not automatic acceptance.","root_link":"Supports same-settings CPU/GPU agreement gate and independent tolerance justification: file:///home/hal/dev/interp/metis/experiments/blind-02/20261103-0124-tree/out/work/seed-20261103/R1/framer.md.","compute_open":"none","sequential":"Proposed minimum: eight instrumented executions, CPU/GPU pair per case, each with about six ordered checkpoints consuming preceding arrays; GPU acquisition reserves one device with unknown busy fraction, while stored-trace analysis uses zero GPU. Cases and rotations can run concurrently if memory, transfers and host capacity permit, while within-rotation dependencies remain; nearest demonstrated implementation distributes docking work: https://www.bi.cs.titech.ac.jp/megadock/perform.html."}
{"id":"I3","title":"Protein-language-model SAE hypothesis audit","summary":"Conditional Goodfire-style branch: inspect biological features, then intervene on activations; Goodfire reports preliminary Evo 2 steering, while InterPLM demonstrates localized protein-feature interventions (https://www.goodfire.com/research/interpreting-evo-2; https://arxiv.org/html/2412.12101v1). Proposed use: follow-up hypotheses.","root_link":"Bears on biological hypotheses behind pose preference, leaving original acceleration, agreement and same-budget quality comparisons unresolved: file:///home/hal/dev/interp/metis/experiments/blind-02/20261103-0124-tree/out/work/seed-20261103/R1/framer.md.","compute_open":"More parallel compute buys features and layers analysed; cited InterPLM v1 studies six ESM-2-8M layers with 10,420 dictionary features each, while above that scope effects on docking quality or interface-hypothesis stability remain unknown: https://arxiv.org/html/2412.12101v1.","sequential":"Using frozen dictionaries, each intervention requires six baseline transformer layers followed by SAE manipulation and downstream layers, roughly fourteen stages uncached; proposed audit needs eight monomer-input slots × (baseline + reconstruction control + F interventions + F random controls), with unknown GPU busy fraction per run. Layers remain ordered, but cached activations permit concurrent intervention branches if memory and batching throughput suffice; nearest causal precedent is InterPLM steering, which does not measure this branching optimization: https://arxiv.org/html/2412.12101v1."}

